---
title: "Architecture"
description: "The logical map: how a save flows from the file watcher to a repainted block in a few milliseconds, and where each crate sits."
---

Saving `post.tmd` updates the browser in a few milliseconds: no reload, no lost
scroll, no cold start. That is possible because of one decision, drawn in
@fig-architecture: **all of the intelligence lives in the Rust core behind a stable
websocket protocol, and the browser preview is a thin client.**

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-architecture
%%| fig-cap: "Thin clients talk to the server over a websocket; all rendering lives in the Rust core."
flowchart LR
  BR["Browser preview<br/>(vanilla JS)"]
  subgraph Server["taliesin server"]
    direction TB
    WS["Websocket<br/>+ file watcher"]
    subgraph Core["taliesin-core"]
      direction TB
      P["Parser<br/>comrak + sourcepos"] --> M["Block model<br/>+ diff"] --> R["Render<br/>page / site / book"]
    end
    K["Warm Jupyter kernel"]
  end
  F[(".tmd files")]
  BR <-->|JSON protocol| WS
  WS --> P
  R -->|changed blocks| WS
  F -.->|watch| WS
  M -->|code cells| K --> M
```

| Component | Crate / path | Responsibility |
|---|---|---|
| Core | `crates/core` | Parsing (comrak + sourcepos), the block model, HTML rendering |
| Server | `crates/server` | Websocket dev server, file watcher, Jupyter kernel pool |
| Web client | `web-client/` | Browser preview client (vanilla JS) |

The protocol is open, so an embedded editor client is a new thin client, not a rewrite:
the in-repo VS Code companion (covered in [extending Taliesin](extending.tmd)) is exactly
that.

## How a save flows

Change `tau` from 10 to 12 in `post.tmd` and save. That edit flows through the
pipeline as @fig-save-flow shows, and crucially, only the **changed** blocks reach
the preview: the heading and the paragraph stay put, and only the plot the cell
redraws crosses the wire.

The order matters, and it is easy to get wrong: a save is **re-render, then
execute, then diff**, not re-render then diff. The server re-runs the render
pipeline (`render/`) to get a fresh block list, then hands that list to the
executor (`exec.rs`), which runs the changed code cells (plus everything
downstream) and **splices each cell's output back into the list as a derived
output block**. Only then does `diff.rs` compare the resulting list against the
live one. Execution sits squarely *between* render and diff: the diff never sees
the bare render output, it sees the render output *after* outputs have been
spliced in. (`{js}` cells are the exception: they run in the browser, never
through `exec.rs`, so a `{js}` block crosses the wire as-is. See
[Interactive cells](rendering.tmd).)

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-save-flow
%%| fig-cap: "A save is re-render → execute → diff: outputs are spliced in before the diff runs."
flowchart LR
  save["Save .tmd"] --> watch["File watcher<br/>(debounced)"]
  watch --> parse["Re-render<br/>(parse + block model)"]
  parse --> exec["Execute cells<br/>(splice output blocks in)"]
  exec --> diff["Diff by block id"]
  diff --> ws["Websocket:<br/>changed blocks only"]
  ws --> dom["Preview updates<br/>in place"]
```

The same save as an interaction over time, module by module, is @fig-save-seq:

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-save-seq
%%| fig-cap: "A save, module by module: only changed blocks (and re-run cells) cross the wire."
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant FS as .tmd file
  participant SV as serve/mod.rs (watcher)
  participant RN as render/ (core)
  participant EX as exec + kernel
  participant DF as diff.rs
  participant CL as client.js
  FS->>SV: changed (notify)
  SV->>RN: re-render (debounced)
  RN->>EX: run first changed cell + downstream
  EX-->>RN: output blocks
  RN-->>SV: new block list
  SV->>DF: new blocks vs live blocks
  DF-->>SV: BlockOps (changed only)
  SV->>CL: ws update / insert / remove
  CL->>CL: swap blocks in place (scroll + live state kept)
```

Read the order off the sequence: `render/` produces a block list, `exec.rs`
consumes *that* list and returns a longer one with output blocks woven in, and
only then does `diff.rs` run. So `RN -->> EX` and `EX -->> RN` are not a render
loop; they are the execution step, and the block list `diff.rs` finally sees is
the post-execution one. This is why an edit to a code cell shows its *new plot*
incrementally: the cell re-runs, its output block re-renders, and the diff swaps
just that output element.

The two crates split cleanly: **`taliesin-core` does all the thinking and touches
no network** (it parses, models, and renders), while **`taliesin-server` is the
long-running process** (CLI, websocket, file watcher, executor, kernel pool). The
browser client is a thin layer that mounts blocks and applies the ops the server
sends.

### One door, two server paths

There is one websocket protocol (`protocol.rs`) and one block model, but two
servers behind them, picked by what you point `preview` at: a single `.tmd` file
or a directory.

| | Single document | Multi-page site / book |
|---|---|---|
| Module | `serve/mod.rs` | `serve_site/mod.rs` |
| You ran | `preview post.tmd` | `preview ./blog` |
| Live state | one `DocState` (the live block list) | one `PageState` **per page** (its own block list + broadcast channel), built lazily on first visit |
| Execution | one `Executor` for the doc | an `ExecPool` of per-page executors drained by a builder worker over a job channel |
| Kernels | one warm kernel per language | bounded warm set (`MAX_WARM_PAGES`, currently 6) by most-recently-built; an evicted page pays a cold start on its next edit |
| A save | rebuilds the doc, diffs, pushes ops | rebuilds **only the affected page**, diffs it, pushes ops to that page's subscribers; a `_site.yml` change re-discovers the site and reloads open tabs |

Both walk the same re-render → execute → diff path per document, and both speak
the same messages (`full_render`, `update` / `insert` / `remove` / `set_meta`,
`diagnostics`, `style`, `reload`, `error`), so the browser client cannot tell
which target it is serving. One server handles a project and a single document
alike (a document resolves to the project it belongs to, or becomes a project of
itself): navigation between pages is an ordinary full page load (no SPA), and only
the visited page keeps a warm kernel. The multi-page subsystem
(page discovery, chrome, cross-page references) lives a layer up in `site/`; see
[The multi-page subsystem](sites.tmd).

## The codebase map

The dependency shape first, who leans on whom (@fig-modules). The server
orchestrates and owns the runtime; the core does all the rendering and never
touches the network; the browser client only mounts blocks and applies ops. Most
arrows are *uses / calls* (a solid arrow), but watch the dashed one: `exec.rs`
does not *call* `diff.rs`. The relationship is **data flow**, not a function
call. `exec.rs` produces the post-execution block list and `serve/mod.rs` /
`serve_site/mod.rs` then hand it to `diff.rs`. The arrow says "the blocks `exec`
produces are what `diff` consumes," which is exactly the re-render → execute →
diff ordering above.

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-modules
%%| fig-cap: "Module dependencies across the three layers. A solid arrow is 'uses / calls'; the dashed exec→diff arrow is data flow (exec produces the blocks diff consumes), not a call."
flowchart TB
  subgraph client["web-client/ · browser (vanilla JS)"]
    CL["client.js<br/>mount · apply ops · click-to-source"]
  end

  subgraph server["crates/server · Taliesin (binary)"]
    direction TB
    MAIN["main.rs · CLI"]
    SRV["serve/mod.rs<br/>single doc"]
    SITE["serve_site/mod.rs<br/>site / book"]
    PR["protocol.rs<br/>shared ws contract"]
    EX["exec.rs · executor"]
    KR["kernel.rs<br/>warm ZMQ kernel"]
    MAIN --> SRV
    MAIN --> SITE
    SRV --> EX
    SITE --> EX
    SRV --> PR
    SITE --> PR
    EX --> KR
  end

  subgraph core["crates/core · taliesin-core (library)"]
    direction TB
    RN["render/<br/>parse → block model → HTML"]
    DF["diff.rs"]
    SM["site/<br/>multi-page projects"]
    INC["includes.rs"]
    FM["frontmatter.rs"]
    CI["cite.rs"]
    MA["math.rs"]
    HL["highlight.rs"]
    RN --> INC
    RN --> FM
    RN --> CI
    RN --> MA
    RN --> HL
    SM --> RN
  end

  CL <-->|ws JSON| SRV
  CL <-->|ws JSON| SITE
  SRV --> RN
  SRV --> DF
  SITE --> SM
  SITE --> DF
  EX -.->|"output blocks<br/>(data flow, not a call)"| DF
```

The data those modules pass around is small (@fig-data-model): a `RenderedDoc` is
a list of `Block`s, an executable block carries a `Cell`, and `diff.rs` turns two
block lists into a stream of `BlockOp`s.

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-data-model
%%| fig-cap: "The core data model (key fields shown): what flows from the parser to the client."
classDiagram
  class RenderedDoc {
    Option~String~ title
    bool toc
    Vec~Warning~ warnings
    Vec~Block~ blocks
  }
  class Block {
    String id
    String sourcepos
    Option~String~ source_file
    String html
    Option~Cell~ cell
  }
  class Cell {
    String lang
    String code
    bool echo
    bool include
  }
  class BlockOp {
    <<enumeration>>
    Update
    Insert
    Remove
    SetMeta
  }
  class Warning {
    String message
    Option~String~ file
    Option~u32~ line
  }
  RenderedDoc "1" *-- "many" Block : blocks
  RenderedDoc "1" o-- "many" Warning : warnings
  Block "1" o-- "0..1" Cell : cell
  Block ..> BlockOp : diff emits
```

With the shape in mind, here is the file-by-file detail, one table per layer.

`crates/core/src`, the library (`taliesin-core`):

| Path | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `render/mod.rs` | The render pipeline (`render_internal`). The coordinator. |
| `render/model.rs` | The block-model data types (`Cell`, `Block`, `RenderedDoc`). |
| `render/page.rs` | Full HTML-page assembly: the `PAGE_TEMPLATE` shell, site-chrome wiring, favicon. |
| `render/emit.rs` | One AST node → HTML (the emitter). |
| `render/divs.rs` | `:::` fenced-div preprocessing + grouping into callouts/columns. |
| `render/figure.rs` | Standalone-image and captioned-cell `<figure>`s. |
| `render/theme.rs` | Theming: light/dark, `.css`/`.scss`, extension themes, the pre-paint script. |
| `render/extension/` | Format extensions (`_extensions/` themes/shortcodes) + shortcode expansion, in a single `mod.rs`. |
| `diff.rs` | Block-level diff (LCS over block ids) → `BlockOp`s. |
| `includes.rs` | `{{< include >}}` resolution + the per-file source map. |
| `math.rs` / `highlight.rs` | Server-side KaTeX / syntect, both offline. |
| `cite/` | Citations + cross-references + the IEEE-style bibliography. |
| `site/` | Multi-page projects: `config` (`_site.yml` model), `book`, `xref` (cross-page refs), `search` (cross-page index), and `mod` (discovery, chrome, page render). |
| `frontmatter.rs` | Front-matter parsing + unknown-key linting (the `closest()` did-you-mean). |
| `render/validate.rs` | Closed-vocabulary linting of cell `#|` options + callout kinds, each a located (click-to-source) `Warning`. |
| `schema.rs` | The bundled Draft-2020-12 JSON Schema for `_site.yml`, shipped in a copy by the VS Code companion. Front matter needs none: no YAML language server reads inside a `.tmd`, so `taliesin lsp` owns it. |

`crates/server/src`, the binary (`taliesin`):

| Path | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `main.rs` | CLI: the `preview` / `build` / `run` / `lsp` / `init` subcommands, plus the retired-verb register. |
| `protocol.rs` | The shared websocket message contract (both servers + the client), defined once so they can't drift. |
| `serve/mod.rs` | Single-document websocket server + file watcher (incl. the `--host` LAN token guard). One `DocState` + one `Executor`. Its `rebuild` runs the re-render → execute → diff path. |
| `serve_site/mod.rs` | Multi-page site/book server (per-page `PageState`, a per-page `ExecPool` drained by a builder worker, cross-page nav, hot reload). Bounds warm kernels to `MAX_WARM_PAGES`. |
| `exec.rs` | Sits *between* render and diff: runs a doc's code cells and splices each cell's output back into the block list as a derived output block (incremental: the first changed cell + everything downstream). |
| `kernel.rs` | The warm Jupyter kernel (ZMQ), reused across edits, with interrupt + timeout. |
| `freeze.rs` | The persistent `_freeze/` execution cache, keyed by a cumulative content hash. |
| `log.rs` | Colourised dev-server console output. |

`web-client/`, the browser preview (vanilla JS, no build step): `client.js`
(mounts blocks, applies ops, the dev menu, click-to-source), `search.js` (the
Cmd-K palette), `toc-spy.js` (the scrollspy). The core also ships JS *assets*
(`code-enhance/` with the `window.taliEnhancers` registry, `mermaid.js`) that
both the live client and the static build inject.

The next two chapters go deeper: [the rendering pipeline](rendering.tmd) (how a
`.tmd` becomes blocks) and [extending Taliesin](extending.tmd) (the conventions,
how to add a feature, and the dev workflow).
